# Nordtronics wildfire MQTT access control.
# Deployed to /etc/mosquitto/acl (see backend/DEPLOY.md).
#
# With an acl_file defined mosquitto denies everything that is not listed
# below, so this is a default-deny policy.

# The ingest worker subscribes to every node's telemetry and may not publish.
user wildfire-ingest
topic read nordtronics/wildfire/+/telemetry

# Task 0095: the ingest worker also reads the base stations' Watch/alert
# events, which it validates and stores. Read only, the same trust level as
# the telemetry read above — it never publishes, and this widens no node's
# access.
user wildfire-ingest
topic read nordtronics/wildfire/+/events

# A base station authenticates with its node id as the username (the MQTT
# username equals the node id the firmware sends on the wire), so these
# patterns give every node read/write access to exactly its own subtree and
# nothing else. `%u` is substituted with the authenticated username.
pattern readwrite nordtronics/wildfire/%u/telemetry

# Uncomment per node if a station ever needs a separate command topic — do not
# widen the pattern above, it would let any node impersonate any other.
# pattern readwrite nordtronics/wildfire/%u/command

# The cabin base station (2026-10-02) publishes telemetry on behalf of its LoRa
# nodes, so it gets an explicit write grant across node subtrees. Node users
# stay constrained to their own %u subtree above; this does not widen theirs.
user base-01
topic write nordtronics/wildfire/+/telemetry

# Alert/watch events from the base station (task 0095). Same trust basis as
# the telemetry grant above: the base speaks for its LoRa nodes.
user base-01
topic write nordtronics/wildfire/+/events
